eration, international human rights, and global citizenship. | S6 P6 SCS second term, week 7; S7 p. 264 | Enrichment | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | Grade-shifted. Named UN bodies and Nigeria’s participation need authoritative fact-checking during authoring. |\n| p5-socs-13 | Cultural Diversity and National Unity | S5 public unified SCS P5; S7 Lagos State MoE legacy P5/P6 Social Studies/Civic | P5 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): diversity, social justice, peace, and unity | Public sources cover cultural diversity/unity, fairness, non-discrimination, tolerance, national unity, patriotism, and peaceful coexistence. | S5 P5 SCS first term, Social Justice; S7 pp. 240–241 and 256–258 | Direct coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | NYSC is a brief-selected national-integration example, not exposed in cited objectives. Apply religious/ethnic neutrality controls. |\n| p5-socs-14 | Leadership and Good Governance | S5 public unified SCS P5; S7 Lagos State MoE legacy P5 Civic/P6 Civic | P5 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): leadership, accountability, and good governance | Public P5 scheme covers leader qualities, responsible leadership, transparency, accountability, rule of law, bad governance, and checks on leaders; legacy scheme names ICPC/EFCC functions. | S5 P5 SCS first and second terms; S7 pp. 27 and 35 | Direct coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | Corruption may be defined age-appropriately; institutional roles must be current and nonpartisan. Neutrality review required. |\n| p5-socs-15 | Revision and Assessment — P5 Social and Citizenship Studies | S5 public unified SCS P5 | P5 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): revision and assessment | Public P5 SCS scheme includes review and examination at the end of each term. | S5 P5 SCS first, second, and third terms, weeks 11–12 | Direct coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | AJAMIX review covers its broader proposed sequence. |\n\n### Primary 6\n\n| Module ID | English title | Curriculum version / actual source used | Grade | Official strand / theme | Official topic or objective / public-source basis | Source page / section | Classification | verificationStatus | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| p6-socs-01 | Globalisation and Its Effects | S6 public unified SCS P6; S7 Lagos State MoE legacy P6; S1 context | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): globalization, trade, culture, and technology | Public P6 SCS covers globalization’s effects on Nigeria, international trade, and cooperation; legacy source covers foreign cultural influence and international trade/technology transfer. | S6 P6 SCS second term, week 4; S7 pp. 255–260; S1 pp. 1–3 | Direct coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | Present opportunities and challenges without ranking cultures. |\n| p6-socs-02 | Human Rights — Deeper Study | S6 public unified SCS P6; S9 blended P6 Social Studies; S7 legacy P6 Civic | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): human rights, responsibilities, and redress | Public P6 sources cover international human rights, basic rights/responsibilities, violations, and protection; legacy civic material links institutions and civil society to rights. | S6 P6 SCS second term, week 7; S9 Social Studies, Human Rights and Responsibilities; S7 pp. 34–36 | Combined coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | Civil/political/economic/social/cultural categories and redress routes are enrichment unless confirmed in the official grade document. Neutrality review required. |\n| p6-socs-03 | Gender Equality and Women's Empowerment | S7 Lagos State MoE legacy P6 Social Studies; S1 context; S6 public unified SCS P6 | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NE